      "item": "def-free-module-on-a-set-and-standard-basis",
      "finding": "The Definition permits an arbitrary unital ring R and says: “The map x↦e_x is the standard basis inclusion.” For the zero ring and X with two distinct elements, every e_x is the same zero tuple, so this map is not injective. The indexed-family construction remains usable in the owned proofs, which do not require this inclusion claim.",
      "severity": "would-be-fatal"
    },
    {
      "item": "def-simple-module",
      "finding": "The Definition first requires “M≠0 and its only submodules are 0 and M”, then says: “Equivalently, M has no proper nonzero submodule.” The zero module satisfies the second clause but fails M≠0, so the asserted equivalence is false as written. The owned finite-module proof uses the nonzero simple-module condition.",
      "severity": "would-be-fatal"
    }
  ],
